{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies","id":1181379,"title":"Drowned Legacies","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-02-01T06:42:04-08:00","id":1181379,"title":"Drowned Legacies","content":"I am rather taken with the small amount of information we have on the Drowned Legacies from Beckett's Jyhad Diary. Have any of you put any work into fleshing them out and making them playable? Or is there any chance that they might get their own book at some point in the future? <br /> <br /> I've put my start of an attempt at a work up for one of them below.<br /> <br /> Titlacauan<br /> The Titlacauan are a Legacy without a clear point of origin, being generally active in high society. Their unique gift gives them power over reflective surfaces, allowing them to peer through reflections, and ultimately pass between them. They believe that they have no singular progenitor, instead citing a group of vampires as responsible for their creation. Their curse links to their Willpower. As the Titlacauan grows close to frenzy, a trail of salty smoke oozes from her shoulders, and head.<br /> <br /> Disciplines: Celerity, Presence, Espejo<br /> <br /> Espejo<br /> * Mirror Sight - Look into one mirror and see out of another mirror that you know the location of.<br /> <br /> ** Mirror Speech - Look into a mirror to see and conduct a two way conversation with someone looking into a different mirror that you know the location of.<br /> <br /> *** Mirror Grasp - Reach into a mirror and have your hand come out through another mirror you know the location of with the ability to pull small objects back to you through the mirror.<br /> <br /> **** Mirror Haven - Step into a mirror to sleep within it in a similar manner to earthbond.<br /> <br /> ***** Mirror Walk - Step into one mirror, step out of another mirror which you know the location of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1456-zeke-hubris","name":"Zeke Hubris","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-02T10:25:42-08:00","id":1181706,"title":"","content":"One Bloodline we created with a friend is the Quetzal line. They share a lot of history with the Tlacique (and may even be a &quot;sister line&quot; to them) and believe they descend from Quetzalcoatl. They have the discipline spread Auspex/Animalism/Protean, with a rainbow rattlesnake snake as a &quot;land&quot; animal form and a Quetzal as a &quot;sky&quot; animal form. They have the Gangrel weakness but usually acquire colorful scales as a result of their frenzies, or have feathers growing into their hair.<br /> <br /> Unfortunatly they disappeared with the conquest of America and one of their last, a spanish dude embraced in the 1490s, was tricked into turning into a Baali a few years after and cannot Embrace for fear of spreading Baali blood instead. He's currently working on a ritual to shed the Baali blood in order to resurrect que Quetzal bloodline, but isn't there yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16128-kalendeer","name":"Kalendeer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-02T11:56:22-08:00","id":1181742,"title":"","content":"Given that we know pre-Columbian vampires in the Americas knew Animalism and Protean, I'd like to nominate another bloodline as a possible Drowned Legacy: the Noiad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-02T13:18:18-08:00","id":1181777,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nosimplehiway</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1181742#post1181742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given that we know pre-Columbian vampires in the Americas knew Animalism and Protean, I'd like to nominate another bloodline as a possible Drowned Legacy: the Noiad.</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering their geography, it's likely that the Noiad's chosen peoples (you know, the ones they drove to extinction by pushing further and further north) would have ended up crossing over to the Americas. Vikings managed it; they just didn't have the same mindset as future settlers, and integrated into the population and their genes/culture were diluted/absorbed (or they mostly went home, hard to say), so no reason the Noiad couldn't end up in the Americas really. Especially if they had something supernatural helping them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-02T15:36:52-08:00","id":1181826,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1181777#post1181777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Considering their geography, it's likely that the Noiad's chosen peoples (you know, the ones they drove to extinction by pushing further and further north) would have ended up crossing over to the Americas. Vikings managed it; they just didn't have the same mindset as future settlers, and integrated into the population and their genes/culture were diluted/absorbed (or they mostly went home, hard to say), so no reason the Noiad couldn't end up in the Americas really. Especially if they had something supernatural helping them.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Of course, we'll find more out about the Noiad when they appear as one of the three playable clans in V5.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#000080\">Matthew Dawkins</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000080\">In-House Developer for Onyx Path Publishing</span></b><br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b><span style=\"color:#008080\">Website: </span><a href=\"http://www.matthewdawkins.com\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008080\">https://www.matthewdawkins.com</span></a><br /> <span style=\"color:#008080\">Patreon: </span><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/matthewdawkins\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008080\">https://www.patreon.com/matthewdawkins</span></a></b></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/152-the-gentleman-gamer","name":"The Gentleman Gamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-02T15:48:23-08:00","id":1181831,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Gentleman Gamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1181826#post1181826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Of course, we'll find more out about the Noiad when they appear as one of the three playable clans in V5.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you continue doing this it will become A Meme.<br /> <br /> New Sect; The Crone's Own<br /> <br /> The wild sect is ruled over by the Lhiannan, the Noiad and the Ahrimane. Other Clans and Bloodlines may exist within the sect (and its territory), but only if they show humility before nature.<br /> <br /> The sect values wisdom and temperance above all else. Koldunic Sorcery (Ogham) and Protean are freely spread among the more spiritual adherents.<br /> <br /> They also have a huge city in the Middle Umbra, where there are several slumbering Antedeluvians (the Crone, Lilith and Ennoia among them) and huge populations of mortals and Cainites. They boast an all-new bloodline, who claim to be the true Lilin heritage of Ennoia (both her biological and sanguine offspring) are all Abominations, but with none of the downsides, and a better version of Protean which pedestrian Protean is a bastardized version of.<br /> <br /> <i>If this is coming off as me REALLY hating the True Black Hand, I promise I don't. I love everything about the TBH and Enoch, the Trujah and the Nagaraja, I just think Dirty Secrets did a very very bad job of selling all of it to be honest.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 02-02-2018, 07:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-03T11:17:01-08:00","id":1182026,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1181831#post1181831\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... and a better version of Protean which pedestrian Protean is a bastardized version of.<br /> [/I]</div> </div> </div> </div>If by better you mean it gets you possessed by alien parasites, yeah!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-03T11:30:28-08:00","id":1182031,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nosimplehiway</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182026#post1182026\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If by better you mean it gets you possessed by alien parasites, yeah!</div> </div> </div> </div>See, I get a lot of people saying that Vicissitude is extremely powerful, but I just don't see it.<br /> <br /> Ignoring the bullshit people think Vicissitude 2 can do which it can't (turn you into an ottoman with a single touch, fleshcraft you without taking a whole turn to grapple you first, heal Kindred despite it never saying it can do this), Vicissitude is pretty average. I love it, but Temporis (DSotBH version) it ain't.<br /> <br /> 1: Appearance 5 for free, also disguise your face and voice if you have quite a lot of time to kill.<br /> 2: Same but works on others. Also very situational and underpowered combat applications.<br /> 3: The freeform parts of it which don't convey many mechanical benefits (reshaping someone who you could easily kill without Vicissitude because they're restrained into something horrible), plus some relatively average combat applications which can be dangerous if you're VERY well speced.<br /> 4: A really powerful transformation power; +3 to each physical stat is just really nice, stack that on top of blood buff and some physical Disciplines and you're a real threat, just not something you can actually use in most fights due to limited space, Masquerade etc.<br /> 5: A less powerful version of mist form.<br /> <br /> Vicissitude really starts to shine when mixed with other powers, when RPed well, when carefully thought about, whereas Protean gives you:<br /> <br /> 1: Intimidation buff, perfect nightsight.<br /> 2: The most accessible aggravated damage that doesn't require a grapple.<br /> 3: An indispensible method of hiding and avoiding death by sunlight.<br /> 4: A really weak buff transformation power (+1 strength and double running speed, wooooooooo), but also access to Flight, many disguises and a lot of different difficulty reductions depending on forms.<br /> 5: A really great incorporeality power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-03T11:39:44-08:00","id":1182032,"title":"","content":"I am interested to see what people think of the Drowned Legacies as written (in brief) in BJD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#000080\">Matthew Dawkins</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000080\">In-House Developer for Onyx Path Publishing</span></b><br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b><span style=\"color:#008080\">Website: </span><a href=\"http://www.matthewdawkins.com\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008080\">https://www.matthewdawkins.com</span></a><br /> <span style=\"color:#008080\">Patreon: </span><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/matthewdawkins\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008080\">https://www.patreon.com/matthewdawkins</span></a></b></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/152-the-gentleman-gamer","name":"The Gentleman Gamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-03T13:58:03-08:00","id":1182062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182031#post1182031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See, I get a lot of people saying that Vicissitude is extremely powerful, but I just don't see it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe the problem is crunch and fluff<sup>1</sup>.<br /> <br /> The crunch side makes Vicissitude kind of a good, <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/EvilGloating\" target=\"_blank\">out of combat</a>, utility discipline, until it hits oooo when it really blossoms into a Lovecraftian death machine. Level 5 is mostly helpful for having highly inaccessible havens. Over-all, it's probably comparable to Protean or Dominate. A tent-pole power for clans that have it. Maybe best used by npcs, but not too gonzo.<br /> <br /> It's when the fluff (especially some of the implied, necessary <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/RequiredSecondaryPowers\" target=\"_blank\">secondary powers</a>) is included that the whole thing spirals out of control.<br /> <br /> You can use Vicissitude to change your shape and the shape of others. Fine. So, why can't you just add or subtract physical merits and flaws to characters? I can give my ghoul a fully functional third arm, sprouting from their left knee, but I can't give them the Bruiser merit? I can change my own appearance, including increasing Appearance (which has drifted away from pure physical beauty since Vicissitude was first written), but still have to pay points for Blush of Health? I can't use Vicissitude to inflict Blind or Lame on a victim, but I can remove their eyes and limbs... which does no actual damage? The answer is, in most campaigns, you can do all these things. If a Tzimisce pc announces that he grabs a rival's ghoul and tears out her eyes and removes her legs, well, most ST would allow Vicissitude to do it. It just makes sense that you could, even though it's not explicitly stated in the rules.<br /> <br /> If you can change fat into muscle, or make bones thicker, why <i>can't</i> you change someone's physical stats, just like Appearance? &quot;Game balance&quot; is not an argument characters in-world would accept as a reason to not do something.<br /> <br /> Also, there are implied applications of Vicissitude that exist because it allows for biologically improbable changes to be made, which last and function smoothly. If you can create a fully functional eye in the back of a human's head, why can't you go to a hospital, find someone who has lost an eye in an accident, and build a replacement for them? Or, attend open heart surgery and just rebuild the heart into a healthy one? Or find a really old person and rebuild their body, into the body of a healthy, twenty-year-old? Sure, all of these things would take some time, and won't be happening on-the-fly in combat. Sure, they would all require the Medicine knowledge, but so does Vicissitude. They are all things that if you read the fluff of Vicissitude should be do-able.<br /> <br /> This all leads to a sort of power creep that just doesn't happen with &quot;I can turn into a bat&quot;. (Despite <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass%E2%80%93energy_equivalence\" target=\"_blank\">conservation of energy/mass</a> saying that should be a <i>highly </i>energetic transformation... at best you'd be a radioactive bat, at worst think &quot;I go boom!&quot;<i>. </i> The coolest possibility, actually, might be a very, very, very fast bat. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The weirdest implication is that when you change from bat back to human the room should get <i>very, very</i> cold.)<br /> <br /> Really, when you consider the full implications of changing any animal form you can see and touch into a new, viable shape, even if it shouldn't be viable, you're almost dealing with mage sphere levels of power and versatility.<br /> <br /> The only other vampiric powers that come close are certain paths of blood sorcery, most notably <a href=\"https://xkcd.com/965/\" target=\"_blank\">Conjuring</a>, Alchemy, and Movement of the Mind. Weather Manipulation would come close if you could convince an ST what it actually <i>means</i> to manipulate ambient energy and matter on that kind of scale.<br /> <br /> Then, of course, as you mention, <a href=\"https://futurism.com/the-physics-of-stopping-time-2/\" target=\"_blank\">Temporis</a> is the most over-powered, at least if the fluff and logical implications of the power are included. The fact that it makes the WoD into a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T-symmetry\" target=\"_blank\">non-time symmetric space</a>... sounds like a small change to the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy_(arrow_of_time)\" target=\"_blank\">laws of physics</a>, but it matters. A lot. Cosmically a lot. Like the Trujah could break the universe. Or, rather, by definition, they already have and then it unbroke, then re-broke, and no one would ever notice, except that there is no such thing as velocity or movement or change or anything, really, because the whole universe becomes one giant Schrodiger's box and we're the cat.<br /> <br /> Temporis makes my head hurt<sup>2</sup>.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <sup>1 </sup>Which would make a truly awesome line of branded and licensed table-top rpg breakfast cereals. I'm picturing crunchy oat squares, and marshmallows in the shape of various dice. Of course, the Hero System players would complain it has too much flavor. And, the D&amp;D players would complain it wasn't as good as their original cereal.<br /> <br /> <sup>2</sup>The topic was... what? Drowned Legacies or something, right? I honestly forget at this point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-03T14:53:21-08:00","id":1182077,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11558_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11558\" data-vbnamecard=\"11558\">Nosimplehiway</a> But the power arises from uncritical application of unrelated powers. Nothing in Vicissitude talks about turning one kind of tissue into another. Nothing in Vicissitude has this vague ability to transform the nature of tissues (dead heart tissue to living and such). They're all things that you might imagine are part of the 1-5 powers but really don't belong there.<br /> <br /> Vicissitude can remove a ghoul's leg, sure. But imagine the actual craft that goes into that. Running your fingernail around their thigh to split the flesh, going down through fascia and muscle, slicing through muscles and tendons and major vessels, as they scream and struggle. Snapping through the bone with level 3; yes it would be a damn sight easier than just tearing their leg off, but it isn't something you can do in a single roll, or a single 3 second (!) turn.<br /> <br /> And Vicissitude does let you move Physical Attributes around, I just glazed over that because it's a system that doesn't get much use. There's definitely potential for saving EXP costs there (Stamina 1 and Strength 5 is a better position for advancing with EXP than Stamina 2 and Strength 4), which is a neat and realistic application. But this is all the domain of downtimes, which is where it shines. Like Dominate 4, you don't make progress in a single scene. If you want an armchair that walks around the house, offering itself to guests, then that's months of work. I mean just imagine the skeletal rearrangement necessary, the training you'd need to give it, how much time you'd have to devote to crushing their spirit... Okay now I'm imagining my Friendly Neighborhood Anarchs (the PCs of the game I ST) accepting a friendly Tzimisce into their ranks, but the first time they visit they find that their house is decked with several mortal and vampiric decorations...<br /> <br /> But while Arnold the Armchair is definitely, definitely possible in both crunch and fluff, it's not something you can whip out in a manner which actually affects balance. If Vicissitude 3 could do in a turn what it does in a year, it would be the most powerful ability in the game to be sure.<br /> <br /> And yes, tangents are all well and good but the American bloodlines do interest me and too much tangent can harm a thread ;-)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-03T16:28:39-08:00","id":1182107,"title":"","content":"Vicissitude can, in canon, create a &quot;chitinous&quot; carapace on a subject. Chitin, a long chain polysaccharide vaguely similar to sugar, is not available as a building block for vertebrates. It is so alien to vertebrates that most of us are allergic to it to some degree. The problem being that it is just close enough to us in the evolutionary tree (belonging to arthropods, fungi and such) that it is bio-available to vertebrates, but alien enough to have sometimes severe health effects. Chitin is why homes with roaches tend to also have people with asthma. Putting a large quantity of chitin on a human would lead to anaphylactic shock. If nothing else, it would be absorbed as really bad sugar, with who knows what health effects.<br /> <br /> That's a foreign tissue existing happily alongside human tissue, with no duration limit. Those are changes on a biochemical level. Serious biohacking.<br /> <br /> As for leg removal, the amazing part isn't that they remove the leg slowly. Humans can do that. It's that they do it without causing <i>any</i> health levels of damage. Major surgery without damage. That's a big deal.<br /> <br /> As for living furniture... I never understood that. It just seems so uncomfortable.<br /> <br /> Vicissitude is powerful. It's not a game-breaker, true, but the Tzimisce definitely won the super-power lottery. (As did the Tremere.)<br /> <br /> And, let me think on relating this all back to native american legacies... this may take some pondering, but I'll get there.<br /> <br /> Weren't there Tzimisce who used flesh-crafting to look like Hopi demons or something?<br /> <br /> In the meantime, do the NatAm legacies increase the evidence for a multi-focal origin for vampires? Do they mean we can finally knock Caine off his basalt pedestal?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-04T00:34:40-08:00","id":1182193,"title":"","content":"Tying together the twin threads of the Drowned Legacies and the Vicissitude discussion here, I can't help but imagine a descendant of the Tzimisce doing exceptionally well in South America. For one, getting there must have come easily enough, or at least easier than some, given the tales of the Eldest swimming about during the Deluge. Furthermore, while the Tlacique have the whole &quot;walking blood gods&quot; thing pretty well under control, I think the bloody rituals across Mesoamerica feels like it would encourage all manner of depravity among Cainites. In fact, it wouldn't surprise me if the wars amongst the natives, especially the extinction of certain civilizations before European colonization, could have been akin to Carthage across the Atlantic. I'm especially attracted to the idea of Baali overseas - some Aztec rituals sound like they'd mesh well with consecrating a well or two. <br /> <br /> In regards to dethroning Caine as the single progenitor, while possible, I feel that there is evidence enough of Kindred being able to sustain themselves for extended water journeys (aquatic Gangrel, for instance), that the narrative could accommodate them, but be vague enough to allow all sorts of details to be filled in by a Storyteller. For instance, I personally explain the strange nature of the Tlacique as them being descended from a Gangrel Methuselah that joined the Cult of Set to curb his more animalistic nature and basically installed himself as a god abroad to spread the dark word, taking the form of the Quetzal with his mastery of Protean. He's long since forgotten, but his descendants have fit into this oddly liminal space between Gangrel and Setite.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13249-sandcarlyle","name":"SandCarlyle","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-04T02:03:14-08:00","id":1182203,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SandCarlyle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182193#post1182193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In regards to dethroning Caine as the single progenitor, while possible, I feel that there is evidence enough of Kindred being able to sustain themselves for extended water journeys (aquatic Gangrel, for instance), that the narrative could accommodate them, but be vague enough to allow all sorts of details to be filled in by a Storyteller. For instance, I personally explain the strange nature of the Tlacique as them being descended from a Gangrel Methuselah that joined the Cult of Set to curb his more animalistic nature and basically installed himself as a god abroad to spread the dark word, taking the form of the Quetzal with his mastery of Protean. He's long since forgotten, but his descendants have fit into this oddly liminal space between Gangrel and Setite.</div> </div> </div> </div>I tend to explain the Tlacique the opposite way: a Follower of Set found their way to the Americas, and embraced a local.<br /> <br /> How that local developed Protean is another matter, and requires us to look at 1st edition Sorcerer. In that book, it maintained that when hedge wizards got embraced, their knowledge of Sorcery could carry over, if the character took a little time and effort to adapt them. While this is most evident with actual Blood Sorcery (both mortal and vampire sorcery use Paths, and both have similar Paths), it can also potentially manifest in other Disciplines. A user of the Path of Fascination, for example, developing dots in Presence.<br /> <br /> The big narrative example the book used, though, was of a South American Sorcerer who had many dots in the Path of Shapeshifting. After the character loses access to their normal spirit talking ability (which they Paradigmatically tie into their ability to shapechange), they work and work and pray and pray, until they spontaneous re-develop the ability to assume the form of a Panther.<br /> <br /> Once I read that, the idea of the Tlacique having been founded by a former Sorcerer turned Setite, who transferred their shapeshifting ability into Protean, became so obvious as to be basically canon. I refuse to believe otherwise, simply because it's the simplest and most elegant solution.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-04T04:02:08-08:00","id":1182222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182203#post1182203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I tend to explain the Tlacique the opposite way: a Follower of Set found their way to the Americas, and embraced a local.<br /> <br /> How that local developed Protean is another matter, and requires us to look at 1st edition Sorcerer. In that book, it maintained that when hedge wizards got embraced, their knowledge of Sorcery could carry over, if the character took a little time and effort to adapt them. While this is most evident with actual Blood Sorcery (both mortal and vampire sorcery use Paths, and both have similar Paths), it can also potentially manifest in other Disciplines. A user of the Path of Fascination, for example, developing dots in Presence.<br /> <br /> The big narrative example the book used, though, was of a South American Sorcerer who had many dots in the Path of Shapeshifting. After the character loses access to their normal spirit talking ability (which they Paradigmatically tie into their ability to shapechange), they work and work and pray and pray, until they spontaneous re-develop the ability to assume the form of a Panther.<br /> <br /> Once I read that, the idea of the Tlacique having been founded by a former Sorcerer turned Setite, who transferred their shapeshifting ability into Protean, became so obvious as to be basically canon. I refuse to believe otherwise, simply because it's the simplest and most elegant solution.</div> </div> </div> </div>I remember something in canon saying that the &quot;Protean&quot; of the Tlacique is just Serpentis, but with a different kind of faith. The power was protean enough (heh) to mutate to reflect the beliefs of a bloodline who have jaguars rather than snakes as a core animistic concept. This is backed up by the Rainbow Serpents of the Serpents of the Light, as one example, and the way the powers varied across Europe in the Dark Ages, with a greek influence leading to a more monstrous aesthetic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-04T06:37:18-08:00","id":1182245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Gentleman Gamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182032#post1182032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am interested to see what people think of the Drowned Legacies as written (in brief) in BJD.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm intrigued by them and would love to read some more fleshed out information about them and how they have managed to stay hidden whilst manipulating the Camarilla and Sabbat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1456-zeke-hubris","name":"Zeke Hubris","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-05T03:42:47-08:00","id":1182534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Gentleman Gamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182032#post1182032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am interested to see what people think of the Drowned Legacies as written (in brief) in BJD.</div> </div> </div> </div>- Aztec symbolism has layers of meaning. &quot;Cipactli&quot; is the primitive water monster, or a caiman. As a sea monster, is wasn't different from a Tiamat. As a caiman it was a symbol for the primitive earth and for the founding of dynasties. I'd describe the Legacy not as reptilian, but as monstrous, and related to power. It wasn't bad written, and I know the writers didn't had enough words to developed the legacies. <br /> <br /> - Karai Pyhare is the &quot;Lord of the Night&quot; from the Guarani speaking peoples of South America. It's a night spirit, and thus any vampire (or non-vampire) could be translated as the &quot;Karai Pyhare&quot;.I don't know why there were described as &quot;bestial&quot;, and I'd like to develop them as tricksters that use local lore to trick the humans into fear or submission. <br /> <br /> - The Kalku from Mapuche lore are witches that deal with evil spirits. It would be interesting to have a new spiritual blood-magic. <br /> <br /> In developing the Legacies I'd consider the following principles:<br /> <br /> - There was shared cultural identity between large areas of Mesoamerica and South America before the arrival of the Spanish and Portuguese. There were large linguistic groups, but even within each linguistic group there were different agricultural and material cultures, imposed by the environment. In the western half of South America, for example, you had the Tupi (and others) living from the rain forest to semi-deserts. <br /> <br /> - Current native lore isn't the best guide to develop the legacies, specially for South America. The peoples from Central America had a writing system, but most South American groups didn't had written languages. It means it is possible to understand the Mexica and Mayan worldviews, but it's almost impossible to know more about the Guarani religion in the 16th century. For example, Guarani lore from Argentina had the word &quot;Luison&quot; for a kind of monster, but this word is just a misappropriation of the Spanish word for werewolf. It's impossible to know (to my knowledge) if the Argentinian Guarani adopted European folklore (as it happened in my own country, Brazil) or if they started using an European word for their own monster.<br /> <br /> - Studying the local culture and conditions if pretty important. It's impossible to use the same developing guidelines used for Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom to develop South American legacies. The African peoples were able to preserve their culture and native religions and languages in a greater degree than Native American peoples, and most Native Americans right now have a mixed heritage. Aboriginal or Amerindian Brazilians, for example, are only 0.4% of the country's population, but almost all Brazilians have some remote (16th-18th century) Amerindian admixture. Our language is European, and our religion is mostly European. Mostly importantly, our worldview is mostly influenced by Southern European/Catholic culture, specially in the Northeastern portion of the country. So, in portraying the &quot;aboriginal legacies&quot;, it's important to study the local peoples before the culture melange the country became after the 16th century and specially after the 17th and 18th centuries. It's the only way to distinguish between truly native influences and foreign influences (foreign being a relative term, since most locals consider Catholicism and Roman languages as their own native heritages, specially when compared to the Protestant English-speaking North).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1119-primal-flame","name":"Primal Flame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1119_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"230"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-05T03:49:57-08:00","id":1182535,"title":"","content":"Also, I'd like to add that it would be interesting to create a Mesoamerican mythology for that region's legacies, somewhat different from aboriginal Human myth, but using the Human myths as its basis. The idea of the Black Sun is really interesting. It embodies the notions of cradle and grave, and cyclical idea of time (like the idea of a cyclical Gehenna).<br /> <br /> Check it here: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sun_(mythology\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sun_(mythology</a>)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1119-primal-flame","name":"Primal Flame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1119_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"230"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T13:58:15-08:00","id":1188293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Gentleman Gamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182032#post1182032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am interested to see what people think of the Drowned Legacies as written (in brief) in BJD.</div> </div> </div> </div>Description was a little short to make up my mind. On the subject of their history, I'd think the extremely fast, by kindred standards near instant depopulation of the Colombian exchange would be important. Multiple diseases racing ahead of the conquistadors like wildfire in virgin fields, populations dropping 90 +%, 100% in some places. The Amazon so depopulated it turned into jungle.More high-population places hit harder. The bottom dropping out of the food web for kindred.<br /> <br /> Oh and I remember people speculating for a log time that Set was embraced into the 4th generation by Ennoia and later diablerized himself to third. The Tlactique would be spawn of his from his wanderings before the diablerie.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2471,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2471-trollroot","user":"Trollroot","date":". 02-21-2018, 05:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2471-trollroot","name":"Trollroot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"311"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T16:25:11-08:00","id":1188331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Trollroot</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1188293#post1188293\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh and I remember people speculating for a log time that Set was embraced into the 4th generation by Ennoia and later diablerized himself to third. The Tlactique would be spawn of his from his wanderings before the diablerie.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's quite interesting. Which books people used as basis for this hypothesis? <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1119-primal-flame","name":"Primal Flame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1119_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"230"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T13:04:30-08:00","id":1188629,"title":"","content":"As far as I remember it started with the first Mummy book, the one that was a VtM supplement. From what I remember (this was decades ago) Set, after his embrace was not markedly more powerful than his brother Osiris. Then he went away for a long while, and came back and just kicked everyones asses at the same time. Mages, werewolves, Osiris. Everyone. He'd had a massive power-up. Diablerie was suspected.<br /> <br /> Later, people noted how similar Serpentis was to Protean and that a lot of Sets snake stuff could be animal features from frenzies.<br /> <br /> Years after this we got the Tlactique, obviously Setites and probably the first branch of the clan. They had Protean rather than Serpentis. <br /> <br /> Gehenna I think showed a Set with a serpent head, a fairly massive animal feature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2471-trollroot","name":"Trollroot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"311"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T13:21:12-08:00","id":1188635,"title":"","content":"As I remember it, the notion that Set diablerized someone was a fairly common but not universally accepted theory. It fitted the facts well and he was the sort to do it mostly. The belief that he'd been embraced by Ennoia... well as I remember it, he went off into the Western Desert and lived on his own there. Warrior sort, surviving on his own in one of the harshest, emptiest places of the world, gets embraced, comes out with a shapeshifting discipline and this animal thing/feature. Spelt Gangrel to people. 10 years later, the Tlactique showed up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2471-trollroot","name":"Trollroot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"311"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-07T13:49:36-07:00","id":1520054,"title":"","content":"Keeping Protean and sprinkling fire via sorcery in Brazil we have the Mula sem Cabe\u00e7a (Headless Donkey) with the body of a donkey and fire coming from the neck. We also have something for Serpentis adepts, the Boitat\u00e1, a tiny serpent with big, fiery eyes. But I guess Boitat\u00e1 would be a Fera, based in their protector of forests status.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27825-mrdrika","name":"MrDrika","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27825_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"64"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-08T01:35:43-07:00","id":1520088,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zeke Hubris</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1181379-drowned-legacies?p=1182245#post1182245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">and how they have managed to stay hidden whilst manipulating the Camarilla and Sabbat.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, keeping attention to mundane details and making sure that basic neccesities, duties, and improvements are done...these mundane factors goes a long way in regard to being safe and having precautions. No need constant use of abilities or supernatural protections, unless they are especially needed, that is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}